---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
import WangcaiSeries from '../../components/WangcaiSeries.astro';
const P = '/misc/wangcai-2025/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Bringing Wangcai Home — Huanyu Chen"
  description="A short winter break in the Northeast after the SIGGRAPH deadline: Boston, Portsmouth and Portland, Maine, and the trip where we got our dog, Wangcai.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · January 2025</p>
  <h1>Bringing Wangcai Home</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A short break after the SIGGRAPH deadline in the Northeast, and the trip where we got our dog.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>4 days · January 24–27, 2025</strong><br />
    Los Angeles → Boston → Portsmouth, New Hampshire → Portland, Maine → Boston → Los Angeles<br />
    three New England states in one snowy weekend · one rental car with a dog in the back · one harbor at night, one at sunset ·
    one Boston evening on foot · one dog named Muffin, then WONHTSAI, now Wangcai
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-overview.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-overview.jpg'} alt="Map of the trip: a flight from Los Angeles to Boston, a drive up the coast to Portland, Maine and back, and a flight home past Seattle." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole weekend. Blue dashes are flights; red dashes are driving.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Every January, a lot of graphics researchers spend the weeks before the SIGGRAPH paper deadline living on coffee and
    compile errors, and then, the moment the submission goes through, go a little blank. This trip came right after one
    of those deadlines. On paper it was just a long weekend in New England: a flight to Boston, a drive up the coast to
    Portsmouth and Portland, Maine, an evening walk through Back Bay, and a flight home. But the reason I went to Boston
    in the coldest week of the year has four legs and a cream-colored coat, and she is asleep on the couch as I write
    this.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other trip posts here, everything below is rebuilt from the camera roll: where each photo was taken,
    and when. This was a small album, about fifty pictures, and it has some long silences in it. Where it is silent,
    I've left the gap rather than fill it in.
  </p>

  <h2>First, Muffin</h2>
  <p>
    The story starts a month earlier, in December 2024. A rescue post came across our screens about a shelter dog called
    <strong>Muffin</strong>, a cream, German-Shepherd-looking girl named for her oat-colored coat. The shelter was full and
    she was due to be euthanized. There was a way out: an adopter in Boston wanted her, but she needed somewhere to stay
    in the meantime, and if no one could take her she would be put down within days.
  </p>
  <p>
    So we took her home as a foster. It was supposed to be about a week, since she already had a family waiting. It was
    a good week. She ate her first meal in our home, discovered the little cups of whipped cream that coffee shops hand
    to dogs and decided they were the best thing in Los Angeles, wore a pink sweater, saw the ocean for the first time
    at the beach, and watched a sunset from a hilltop in the Hollywood Hills.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-pier.jpg'} alt="A cream-colored dog in a pink knit sweater looking out through a pier railing at the ocean and a sandy beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Her first look at the ocean, during her foster week in LA, December 2024.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-hollywood-hills.jpg'} alt="The same dog in a pink sweater and a teal harness at a railing high in the Hollywood Hills, with the Hollywood sign on the ridge behind in late sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset in the Hollywood Hills, the same week.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Within days she was sleeping belly-up on the couch, which in dog terms is a signed lease. Then the week was over. The last photo of her in LA shows her in a
    travel crate in the car, surrounded by all her toys. She was the first dog we had ever raised.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-couch.jpg'} alt="The dog asleep upside down on a tan leather couch, still in her pink sweater, one front paw in the air." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The signed lease. Foster week, December 2024.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    She arrived in a snowy Boston, and for a while that was the end of the story. Then it wasn't. Her new home didn't
    work out, and by January she was at the <strong>MSPCA</strong>'s shelter in Boston, up for adoption again. Her
    listing on Adopt-a-Pet opened with "My name is WONHTSAI!", which, read aloud, is very nearly
    <strong>Wangcai</strong> (旺财), the most classic name a Chinese family can give a dog. It means, roughly,
    "prosperous fortune". We took that as a sign.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trouble was that I was in Los Angeles, rushing to finish a SIGGRAPH paper, and that deadline does not move for
    anyone. So the other half of this story's "we" flew to Boston about a week ahead of me and took Wangcai out of the
    shelter, while I finished the deadline in LA. The moment the paper was in, I booked a flight to Boston.
  </p>

  <h2>Friday: Boston by night</h2>
  <p>
    I flew out of LAX around lunchtime on Friday, January 24th, and by evening the moving map had me over southern
    Wisconsin. A little before ten that night we were standing on the <strong>East Boston</strong> waterfront, in
    snow. East Boston is the part of the city that sits across the harbor, next to the airport, and from its piers you
    get the downtown skyline the way the postcards do: the whole Financial District lit up and doubled in the black water
    of the Inner Harbor, with the pointed clock tower of the Custom House among the glass. The paths were edged with old
    snow, the granite bollards had little caps of it, and the harbor was flat and still.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'east-boston-skyline.jpg'} alt="The downtown Boston skyline lit up at night across the dark Inner Harbor, seen from a snowy waterfront path in East Boston." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Boston from East Boston, 9:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'east-boston-zakim.jpg'} alt="A snowy harbor walk at night with the lit towers of the Zakim Bridge in the far distance across the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Turning left: the Zakim Bridge, far off, glowing blue and white.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Turn a little to the left and the view runs up the harbor toward Charlestown, where the white cables of the
    <strong>Zakim Bridge</strong> fan out from their towers like the strings of a harp. It opened in 2003 as part of
    the Big Dig, Boston's famously long and famously expensive project to bury the elevated highway that used to cut
    the city off from its waterfront. At night it is lit blue, and from across the harbor it looks like a ship's rigging
    standing on the water. After a day on a plane, it was a good first look at a city we were about to get to know
    a little.
  </p>

  <h2>Saturday: up the coast</h2>
  <p>
    Saturday was the big day on the road: a loop through Boston's university district in the late morning, then an hour
    north on I-95 to New Hampshire, across the river into Maine, and up the coast to Portland in time for sunset. The sky
    stayed a hard, cloudless blue the whole way, the kind of blue you only get on very cold days, and the snow that had
    fallen earlier in the week was still piled everywhere. We had rented a car, and Wangcai came along for the whole
    loop, riding in the back.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0125.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0125.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: from the East Boston waterfront and Boston University north to Portsmouth, across the Piscataqua River Bridge, and on to Portland, Maine." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 1–2: from the East Boston waterfront to Portland's Old Port.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Commonwealth Avenue</h3>
  <p>
    At eleven we were driving along <strong>Commonwealth Avenue</strong> through Boston University, where the Green Line
    trolleys run down the middle of the road and the campus is strung out along it for over a mile. Two towers own the
    view. One is the <strong>Prudential Tower</strong>, Back Bay's tall gray landmark since the 1960s. The other is new
    and very hard to miss: BU's <strong>Center for Computing &amp; Data Sciences</strong>, opened in 2022, a stack of
    glass boxes set slightly off-center from one another, so the whole building looks like a game of Jenga halfway
    through. That is in fact its nickname. It is also designed to run without fossil fuels, heated and cooled by
    geothermal wells drilled deep under the sidewalk.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'commonwealth-ave.jpg'} alt="Commonwealth Avenue in Boston under a blue sky, with the stacked glass boxes of BU's data sciences building and the Prudential Tower behind traffic lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Commonwealth Avenue: the "Jenga building" and the Prudential.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bu-data-sciences.jpg'} alt="BU's stacked glass tower rising behind an ornate Victorian stone mansion with bay windows, above a snowy bank." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Old Boston and new BU, on Bay State Road.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Around the corner on Bay State Road, the same tower rises right behind a Victorian stone mansion with rounded bay
    windows and a steep slate gable, the kind of house that lines this street by the dozen and that BU has long since
    absorbed as dorms and offices. Seen from the snowbank below, the two buildings look like they are having an argument
    about what Boston should look like, from more than a century apart.
  </p>

  <h3>Portsmouth</h3>
  <p>
    By one o'clock we were in <strong>Portsmouth, New Hampshire</strong>. New Hampshire has only about eighteen miles of
    coastline, and Portsmouth makes the most of them. It was settled in 1623, which makes it one of the oldest towns in
    the country, and its center still feels like a seaport that got rich in the age of sail and then simply stayed put.
    <strong>Market Square</strong> is the heart of it: rows of red-brick shopfronts with dormer windows, cast-iron lamp
    posts, and the tall white steeple of <strong>North Church</strong>, which has been the town's landmark since the
    1850s and shows up in almost every picture of the place, including ours. We warmed up with coffee and pastries
    off the square.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'portsmouth-north-church.jpg'} alt="Market Square in Portsmouth, New Hampshire: brick storefronts and the tall white steeple of North Church against a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Market Square and North Church, Portsmouth, 1:03 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The square was bright and sharp-shadowed, with snow shoveled into heaps at the curbs. A little after two we were at the north end of town, by the
    <strong>Piscataqua River</strong>, which is the border between New Hampshire and Maine. It is a short, wide tidal
    river with one of the strongest currents on the East Coast. On its bank the snow lay thick and unbroken, a plume of
    white steam rose from the riverside industry, and beyond it stood the green steel arch of the
    <strong>Piscataqua River Bridge</strong>, which carries I-95 into Maine.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'piscataqua-bridge-portsmouth.jpg'} alt="The green steel arch of the Piscataqua River Bridge beyond a snowy riverbank, with white steam rising into a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Piscataqua River Bridge from the Portsmouth side.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'piscataqua-bridge-crossing.jpg'} alt="View through a windshield of the interstate curving up onto the green arch of the Piscataqua River Bridge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A minute later, driving onto it. Next stop: Maine.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A minute later we were on it. The bridge opened in 1972, and from the driver's seat it is mostly a green lattice of
    girders sweeping overhead, and then you are in Kittery, Maine. Three states before three o'clock.
  </p>

  <h3>Portland at sunset</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Portland, Maine</strong> announced itself the way port cities do, from the back: a wall of shipping containers
    stacked five high behind a fence on the edge of town, red and blue and rust-brown, many of them stenciled
    <em>Eimskip</em>. That is the Icelandic shipping line, which since 2013 has made Portland its American port of
    call, so a fair amount of what travels between the United States and Iceland passes through this fence line.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'portland-containers.jpg'} alt="Shipping containers stacked high behind a fence at Portland's marine terminal under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Portland's container terminal, from the car.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By four we were on the waterfront, and the light was going gold. <strong>Portland Pier</strong> is a working pier,
    not a pretty one, which is what makes it pretty. Fishing boats were tied up below, decks crowded with coiled line,
    plastic crates and stacks of red-and-white buoys on poles. A heavy rope hung across the view. Out on the water, a
    scatter of birds rode the swell, and across Casco Bay the white oil tanks of South Portland caught the last of the
    sun. Behind us, the wooden decking was buried under drifts of snow that nobody had bothered to shovel.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'portland-pier-buoys.jpg'} alt="A lobster boat with red buoys and coiled line moored below Portland Pier, the calm harbor and South Portland's white tanks beyond under a pale sunset sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Portland Pier, 4:14 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'portland-pier-snow.jpg'} alt="A snow-covered wooden pier at dusk, with sailboat masts, lamp posts and a large white ferry silhouetted against a yellow sunset." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Snowdrifts on the pier, and the sun going down behind the masts.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Next door is <strong>Custom House Wharf</strong>, named after the granite custom house a block inland, a reminder of
    the days when Portland was one of the busiest ports in New England. Its far end is an empty wooden deck with a few
    snowed-in picnic tables, a railing, and nothing between you and the bay. A gull went over while we stood there. The
    sky was clear, pale blue fading to peach at the horizon, and the water had gone the steely blue it turns just before
    dark. It was the prettiest quarter of an hour of the trip.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'custom-house-wharf.jpg'} alt="An empty wooden deck at the end of Custom House Wharf, with a gull flying over Casco Bay under a clear sky turning peach at the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The end of Custom House Wharf, 4:15 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Old Port, and the white strawberries</h3>
  <p>
    After dark the waterfront turns into the <strong>Old Port</strong>, Portland's old warehouse district. Most of it
    dates from just after the Great Fire of 1866, which started on the Fourth of July and burned out a third of the
    city; Portland rebuilt in brick and granite, and those four-story Victorian blocks are what line Fore and Commercial
    Streets today. That night their façades were hung with big glowing globes of green, blue and white lights, left up
    from the holidays, and the red of traffic lights and taillights streaked through the photos, which were all taken
    from a moving car.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'old-port-lights.jpg'} alt="Brick buildings in Portland's Old Port at night, decorated with large glowing balls of green, blue and white lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Holiday lights in the Old Port, 5:38 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In Portland there were lobster rolls, but the rest of Saturday's photos are of fruit. Somewhere along the way we had acquired a clamshell of white strawberries,
    and that evening they were photographed from every possible angle, with the seriousness normally reserved for
    cathedrals. White strawberries are not unripe red ones; they are varieties bred to stay pale, and the giveaway that
    they are ready is the blush on the skin and the red of the seeds. One berry, shaped exactly like a molar, got its
    own portrait session after midnight. A single oyster got the same treatment.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'white-strawberries.jpg'} alt="A close-up of pale white strawberries with red seeds in a clear plastic box." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>White strawberries, examined thoroughly.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'oyster.jpg'} alt="A single rough-shelled oyster sitting on a wooden surface." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One oyster, for scale.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Sunday: Back Bay and the Charles</h2>
  <p>
    Sunday was the drive back: from Portland down the coast to Boston, with Wangcai in the back seat again. The daylight
    hours left no photos at all. By a quarter to six it was dark and we were in Boston, at <strong>Christian Science
    Plaza</strong> in Back Bay, one of the strangest and most impressive public spaces in the city.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0126.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0126.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: from Portland, Maine down the coast to Boston, from Christian Science Plaza to the Harvard Bridge on foot, then across the harbor to Logan Airport." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: back down the coast from Portland, then Back Bay and the Charles at dusk, then Logan.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The plaza is two centuries jammed together. On one side is <strong>The Mother Church</strong>, the headquarters of
    the Christian Science movement: a small Romanesque church of rough granite from 1894, with a stained-glass rose
    window that glows like a jewel at night, joined to a much larger domed extension from 1906. On the other side is
    pure 1970s. In that decade I. M. Pei's firm, with Araldo Cossutta as lead designer, cleared the surrounding
    blocks and built a vast paved plaza around the church, with a tall, thin concrete Administration Building
    and a reflecting pool almost seven hundred feet long. Over the top of it all looms 111 Huntington Avenue, whose
    lit crown has earned it the nickname "the R2-D2 building".
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mother-church.jpg'} alt="The granite Mother Church at night with its glowing stained-glass rose window, and the lit crown of a skyscraper behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Mother Church's rose window, and 111 Huntington's crown.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'christian-science-plaza.jpg'} alt="Christian Science Plaza at night: a long reflecting pool, the tall concrete Administration Building, and trees wrapped in white lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The reflecting pool and the Administration Building, 5:50 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    At blue hour in January, the whole thing is wonderfully theatrical. The pool was a sheet of reflected lights, the
    trees along it were wrapped trunk to tip in white bulbs, the concrete tower glowed from within, and the plaza was
    so big and so empty that the few people crossing it looked like extras on a set. At its far end sits the Sunday
    School building, a low drum of concrete with a fluted canopy curving over its glass front. Peeking around its edge
    is a red "BSO" sign: Symphony Hall, home of the Boston Symphony Orchestra, is just across the street.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sunday-school.jpg'} alt="The rounded concrete Sunday School building at the end of Christian Science Plaza at night, with a fluted canopy over its glass front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Sunday School building, with Symphony Hall's sign beyond.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Harvard Bridge</h3>
  <p>
    From there we walked north up Massachusetts Avenue, and twenty minutes later we were out on the
    <strong>Harvard Bridge</strong>, which crosses the Charles River from Back Bay to MIT. It is the longest bridge over
    the Charles, and it is the most wind-swept place in Boston on a January night. It is also famous for the strangest
    unit of measurement in America. In 1958, MIT fraternity pledges measured its length using one of their own, Oliver
    Smoot, laying him down end to end over and over across the bridge. It came to 364.4 smoots, "plus or minus one ear",
    and the marks are still painted on the sidewalk and repainted every year. (Smoot, wonderfully, went on to become
    chairman of the American National Standards Institute.)
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'charles-zakim.jpg'} alt="The Charles River at night from the Harvard Bridge, with the lit Boston and Cambridge skyline and the Zakim Bridge in the distance reflected in the dark water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downstream from the Harvard Bridge, 6:10 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'harvard-bridge.jpg'} alt="The long sidewalk of the Harvard Bridge at night leading back toward Back Bay, with the Prudential Tower lit in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The walk back toward the Prudential.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Looking downstream, the river opens into the wide Charles River Basin, with Beacon Hill and the towers of Cambridge
    lit up on either side and, at the very end, the white tent of the Zakim Bridge again, the same bridge we had seen
    from across the harbor on the first night. A long column of light lay on the water. Looking back the other way, the
    sidewalk ran straight and empty toward Back Bay, with the Prudential lit at the top. It was a fine way to say
    goodbye to Boston.
  </p>
  <p>
    The last record of the night is from <strong>Logan Airport</strong>, at a quarter to eleven: empty check-in
    lanes, rows of steel stanchions on a shiny floor, and the trip home about to begin.
  </p>

  <h2>Monday: home</h2>
  <p>
    The way home went through Seattle, with a short connection there. At 9:43 the next morning we were high over the foothills of the
    Cascades, looking down on <strong>Lake Washington</strong>, the long lake between Seattle and the Eastside, with the
    towers of downtown Bellevue on its shore and Mercer Island in the middle. The lake is crossed by floating bridges,
    which really do float, on huge concrete pontoons, because the lake is too deep and too soft-bottomed for ordinary
    piers. Puget Sound and its islands spread out behind, all the way to the horizon. In the next photo, a
    frost of ice crystals had grown across the window, so the whole view looked as if it had been etched in glass.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lake-washington.jpg'} alt="Aerial view of Lake Washington, Mercer Island and downtown Bellevue, with Puget Sound and its islands to the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lake Washington and Bellevue, 9:43 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'frosted-window.jpg'} alt="Lake Washington and its floating bridges seen through an airplane window covered in frost crystals, with a wingtip above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The floating bridges, through a frosted window.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A little after two in the afternoon we were coming down over Los Angeles, and the descent was a tour of the whole
    basin in eight minutes.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0127.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0127.jpg'} alt="Map of the descent into LAX: over the Malibu coast and Pacific Palisades, across to downtown Los Angeles, then past SoFi Stadium." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: the descent into LAX, in eight minutes.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The plane crossed the coast over <strong>Malibu</strong>, where the Santa Monica Mountains drop straight into the
    Pacific in a series of steep, folded ridges, with the Pacific Coast Highway threaded along the bottom. Then came
    <strong>Pacific Palisades</strong>, its neighborhoods laid out along the ridges and canyons above the beach. The
    Palisades fire had swept through these hills and streets less than three weeks earlier, on January 7th, and burned
    thousands of homes. The photos are taken from too high to show much detail. But it was impossible to look down
    at that coastline that week and think about anything else.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'malibu-coast.jpg'} alt="Aerial view from a plane of the Santa Monica Mountains' brown ridges dropping to the coast highway and the Pacific at Malibu, with the wing above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Santa Monica Mountains meet the sea, 2:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pacific-palisades.jpg'} alt="Aerial view of Pacific Palisades: neighborhoods along ridges and canyons above the beach and the coast highway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pacific Palisades, less than three weeks after the fire.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then the plane swung east and around, the usual way into LAX, which means passing high over
    <strong>downtown</strong>, its towers looking like a model at this height, before turning back west for the final
    approach. On the way in, it flew right over <strong>SoFi Stadium</strong> in Inglewood, the home of the Rams and the
    Chargers. The stadium sits almost directly under LAX's flight path, which is why it was built sunk deep into the
    ground: its huge translucent roof had to stay low enough for planes like ours to pass overhead. From a window seat,
    you see it the way the architects must have drawn it, as a single sail of a roof floating over the field.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'downtown-la.jpg'} alt="Aerial view of downtown Los Angeles' towers and street grid from a descending plane." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Los Angeles, 2:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sofi-stadium.jpg'} alt="SoFi Stadium's translucent roof seen through an airplane window just below the wing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>SoFi Stadium, four minutes from landing.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Wangcai</h2>
  <p>
    The camera roll from this trip has harbors and steeples and strawberries, and only a few private seconds of video of
    the reason I went. But the story itself is not in doubt. Muffin, otherwise known as WONHTSAI, came back to us from the MSPCA in
    Boston, and she spent that weekend in the back of a rental car, touring three states she will not remember. When
    she saw us again, she jumped on us like an old friend.
  </p>
  <p>
    She has been <strong>Wangcai</strong> ever since. She has a pair of yellow pajamas, which settles the question of
    where she lives. A fortune-bringing name is a lot to live up to. So far she is managing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Wangcai today</h2>
  <p>
    Since she is missing from every photo above, here she is on some of the road trips she has taken since. None of
    these are from the January trip.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-desert-hands.jpg'} alt="The cream-colored dog sitting on desert sand in a harness, with a row of giant white hand sculptures behind a wire fence at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Among the giant hands on Coyote Valley Road, in California's high desert, July 2025.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-bisti.jpg'} alt="The dog lying stretched out on gravel in low golden sunlight, eyes closed, next to a water bowl." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A roll in the dirt at the Bisti/De-Na-Zin trailhead, New Mexico, March 2026.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    She has turned out to be a natural on the road, and the back seat of a car in a strange state seems to suit her
    just fine.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
